+      "kind": "cites",
+      "rationale": "From: \"In a finite unit-conductance network between an origin and a target boundary, if m distinct singleton edge cuts separate them, effective resistance is at least m. Every unit flow has current of magnitude one through each cut edge, so its energy is at least m. More generally pairwise edge-disjoint separating cutsets C_j give the lower bound sum_j 1/|C_j|. This alone provides no random IIC bottleneck tail estimate.\" To: \"Orient a diamond as 0->a,b->c and let simple random walk use its underlying undirected unit-edge graph. Its probability of returning to 0 at two steps is 1/2, although a forward-only walk has return probability zero. Thus bond orientation in oriented percolation does not justify forbidding backward steps in the undirected random-walk spectral-dimension problem.\"."
+    },
